From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller: node identity invariants
Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

Enforce the two invariants that make half-built nodes impossible.

The duplicate-node defect class exists because node identity is tracked by two
structures that disagree: mac_table holds exactly one entry per AL-MAC (on
collision the existing entry wins and e->data is not overwritten), while
devlist/agentlist accept duplicates. Every path that populates a node goes
through cntlr_find_node() -> mactable_lookup(); every consumer iterates the
list. A duplicate is therefore visible to consumers but unreachable by anything
that would fill it in, and stays permanently half-built - which is how np ends
up NULL and the controller takes SIGSEGV in cntlr_check_config_diff().

Two invariants, enforced where the state is actually created:

1. A node is never born half-built. cntlr_alloc_node() binds n->np at creation
   instead of leaving it NULL.

2. Membership in agentlist implies reachability via mac_table. cntlr_mark_agent()
   refuses to promote a node that is not the object mac_table points at, and
   refuses one with no policy binding. Such a node stays in devlist where ageout
   reaps it, instead of becoming visible to consumers that cannot handle it.

cntlr_register_agent() had to be reordered: it promoted the node before creating
its UCI section, so with invariant 2 in place every freshly onboarded agent would
trip the check and inflate nodes_without_policy during entirely normal operation.
That would have destroyed the counter as a signal - it must stay 0 in healthy
operation or the reliability gate is meaningless. cntlr_resync_config() promotes
the node itself once np is bound, so the trailing mark is idempotent.

Note this is not the full lockdown originally intended. Making raw alloc
unreachable from outside was rejected: the only alternative entry point,
cntlr_add_node(), also emits backhaul- and AP-capability queries, so using it
from db_restore would make the controller blast queries at nodes that may not be
online. Instead of making raw alloc uncallable, its result can no longer be
half-built. Still outstanding: one persistence source (UCI and mapc.db both own
node identity today) and restore that enriches rather than creates.

Lab-verified both directions:
  normal onboarding      -> num_nodes=1, nodes_without_policy=0, UCI section created
  orphan in DB, no UCI   -> nodes_without_policy=1, promotion refused, no SIGSEGV

--- a/src/cntlr.c
+++ b/src/cntlr.c
@@ -1025,7 +1025,11 @@
 	n->cntlr = c;
 	n->depth = -1;
 	n->scan_supported = true;
-	n->np = NULL; //c->cfg.apolicy;
+	/* INVARIANT: a node is never half-built. Bind its policy at birth so no
+	 * consumer can ever meet a node object without one. If the UCI section
+	 * does not exist yet (normal onboarding creates it right after), the
+	 * node stays in devlist and cntlr_mark_agent() refuses to promote it. */
+	n->np = cntlr_config_get_node_by_mac(&c->cfg, almacaddr);
 	memcpy(n->almacaddr, almacaddr, 6);
 	n->map_profile = MULTIAP_PROFILE_1;
 
@@ -1048,11 +1052,37 @@
 
 void cntlr_mark_agent(struct controller *c, struct node *n)
 {
+	struct macaddr_entry *e;
 	char ev_data[256] = {0};
 
 	if (node_is_agent(n))
 		return;
 
+	/* INVARIANT: membership in agentlist implies reachability via mac_table.
+	 * mac_table holds one entry per AL-MAC; a node object that is not the one
+	 * it points at is a duplicate and can never be backfilled (every path that
+	 * populates a node goes through cntlr_find_node -> mactable_lookup). Such
+	 * an object must not enter agentlist: consumers iterate the list, so it
+	 * would be visible but permanently unpopulated. Leave it in devlist and
+	 * let ageout reap it. */
+	e = mactable_lookup(c->mac_table, n->almacaddr, MAC_ENTRY_ALID);
+	if (!e || e->data != (void *)n) {
+		c->nodes_without_policy++;
+		warn("%s: refusing to promote non-owner duplicate " MACFMT " - INVARIANT VIOLATED (count=%u)\n",
+		     __func__, MAC2STR(n->almacaddr), c->nodes_without_policy);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	if (!n->np) {
+		n->np = cntlr_config_get_node_by_mac(&c->cfg, n->almacaddr);
+		if (!n->np) {
+			c->nodes_without_policy++;
+			warn("%s: refusing to promote " MACFMT " without policy - INVARIANT VIOLATED (count=%u)\n",
+			     __func__, MAC2STR(n->almacaddr), c->nodes_without_policy);
+			return;
+		}
+	}
+
 	n->is_agent = true;
 	list_del(&n->list);
 	list_add(&n->list, &c->agentlist);
--- a/src/cntlr_map.c
+++ b/src/cntlr_map.c
@@ -344,14 +344,21 @@
 {
 	char macstr[18] = {0};
 
-	cntlr_mark_agent(c, n);
-
-	if (cntlr_config_get_node_by_mac(&c->cfg, n->almacaddr))
-		return;
+	/* Create the policy section BEFORE promoting. cntlr_mark_agent() now
+	 * refuses nodes without a policy binding, and promoting first would make
+	 * every freshly onboarded agent trip that check on its way through - the
+	 * nodes_without_policy tripwire would then count normal onboarding and
+	 * stop being a usable signal.
+	 *
+	 * cntlr_resync_config() binds np and promotes the node itself, so the
+	 * mark below is a no-op in that case (it is idempotent). */
+	if (!cntlr_config_get_node_by_mac(&c->cfg, n->almacaddr)) {
+		snprintf(macstr, sizeof(macstr), MACFMT, MAC2STR(n->almacaddr)); /* flawfinder: ignore */
+		cntlr_config_add_node(&c->cfg, macstr);
+		cntlr_resync_config(c, true);
+	}
 
-	snprintf(macstr, sizeof(macstr), MACFMT, MAC2STR(n->almacaddr)); /* flawfinder: ignore */
-	cntlr_config_add_node(&c->cfg, macstr);
-	cntlr_resync_config(c, true);
+	cntlr_mark_agent(c, n);
 }
 
 static void cntlr_mark_agent_if_supported(struct controller *c, uint8_t *almacaddr,
